## The idea

This is normal level-by-level BFS. The only twist is direction: collect each level left to right as usual, and **reverse every other level** before saving it.

## Step by step

1. Queue the root.
2. For each level, take exactly `queue.Count` nodes, collecting values and queuing children.
3. If this is an odd level, reverse the list.
4. Save it and flip the direction flag.

## Worked example

**Input:** tree [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| level 0 | [3] |
| level 1 | collected [9, 20] → reversed [20, 9] |
| level 2 | [15, 7] |

**Result:** [[3], [20, 9], [15, 7]]

## Cost

O(n) time; O(width) memory for the queue.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

